                <pb facs="0290" n="238" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve11p2/pg238"/>

                <head>83. Backchannel Message From President Nixon to Brazilian President Médici<anchor n="1" xml:id="d83-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1973-03-06"
                        >March 6, 1973</date>.</dateline>
                <p>Please bring the following message from President Nixon exclusively to the attention of President Medici.</p>
                <p>There are indications that Venezuela and Ecuador may seek to have sanctions
                    against Cuba lifted when the General Assembly of the OAS convenes in Washington in April. President Nixon wants President Médici to know that he strongly opposes such a
                    move and that the United States will take vigorous action in opposition if such
                    a proposal is made. Anything President Médici is able to do in support of the United States position
                    will be deeply appreciated.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d83-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d83-f1-content">
                <p><seg type="summary">Summary: Nixon
                        informed Médici he strongly opposed
                        an effort by Venezuela and Ecuador to lift OAS sanctions on Cuba.</seg></p>
                <p><seg type="source">Source: National Archives, Nixon Presidential Materials,
                            NSC Files, Box 772, Country Files,
                        Latin America, Brazil, Vol. IV, 1973–1974. Top Secret; Exclusively Eyes
                        Only. On April 18, Scowcroft informed
                            Kissinger that Nixon had “reacted strongly to an item
                            [<hi rendition="#italic">text not declassified</hi>] regarding
                        Venezuela’s and Ecuador’s efforts to remove OAS sanctions from Cuba. Scowcroft attached a copy of the item with Nixon’s handwritten note,
                        “K[issinger]—inform State <hi rendition="#italic">now</hi>—we are to
                        vigorously oppose this move—get our position in the private channel to
                            Médici.” (Ibid.)</seg></p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
